  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Thunderstorms are in the forecast through the week for much of\n  western Montana and portions of central Idaho. Storms will\n  continue in southwest Montana through the weekend. \n\n- Drying trend through the weekend\n\n\nSatellite imagery early this morning indicates a broad ridge of \nhigh pressure over the central U.S. and a low pressure circulation\noff the coast of the Pacific Northwest. The Northern Rockies is \nstuck in the middle, under southwest flow aloft. Two visible \nmoisture sources are also visible on satellite imagery, one feed \nof Pacific moisture from the southwest, and another feed of \nmonsoonal moisture from the south. As a result, precipitable \nwater stays above average for the entire forecast area going into \nthe weekend. With little large-scale forcing for ascent, this \nmoisture feed will remain in the form of clouds and increased \nrelative humidity. \n\nSmaller scale ascent in the form of convection will bring some \nchances for precipitation. While ample shear and moisture will be \navailable, clearing skies early in the day could create enough \ninstability to fuel afternoon/evening thunderstorms. A dry lower \natmosphere still indicates that the biggest threat from \nthunderstorms will be erratic, gusty outflow winds. Lightning, \nbrief heavy rain, and small hail will also be present with any \nstorm that is able to form this afternoon. \n\nThe potential for afternoon thunderstorms will remain through at\nleast Friday. On Saturday, the Pacific low pressure system moves \nnorth of our region and the flow flattens to a drier, westerly \nflow with decreased instability. The chances of afternoon showers \nand thunderstorms may be confined to southwest MT. Additionally, \nas the gradient between low pressure in Canada and high pressure \nridge in the desert southwest increases, expect a breezy \nafternoon.",
